report, that's just like,

00:11:41.567 --> 00:11:43.710
"Northerner terrorizes Londoners

00:11:43.710 --> 00:11:44.805
by saying hello."

00:11:44.805 --> 00:11:47.388
(all laughing)

00:11:48.420 --> 00:11:50.358
Literally what happened.
- Oh yeah.

00:11:50.358 --> 00:11:51.990
People would...

00:11:51.990 --> 00:11:53.810
Okay, when they bump into
you, right in Japan, right?

00:11:53.810 --> 00:11:54.939
- Yeah.
- And I've totally forgotten

00:11:54.939 --> 00:11:55.772
this was the thing.

00:11:55.772 --> 00:11:57.373
This is the longest cable on earth, man.

00:11:59.360 --> 00:12:02.340
When people bump into you
in Japan, they say sorry.

00:12:02.340 --> 00:12:03.173
- Yeah, yeah.

00:12:03.173 --> 00:12:04.006
- Someone bumped into me,

00:12:04.006 --> 00:12:05.877
and those dickheads didn't say sorry.

00:12:05.877 --> 00:12:06.710
- They didn't say anything.

00:12:06.710 --> 00:12:08.110
- I could not, every
time that happened to me,

00:12:08.110 --> 00:12:09.166
I could not stop saying

00:12:09.166 --> 00:12:10.895
(speaks in foreign language)

00:12:10.895 --> 00:12:11.728
(Connor and Joy laugh)

00:12:11.728 --> 00:12:12.680
It was just like muscle memory.

00:12:12.680 --> 00:12:13.823
Even if I hadn't bumped into someone,

00:12:13.823 --> 00:12:15.500
I was like on the tune.

00:12:15.500 --> 00:12:17.635
And I was like (speaking
in foreign language)

00:12:17.635 --> 00:12:19.570
I was like, "I'm not in Japan anymore.

00:12:19.570 --> 00:12:21.850
I need to stop doing
this 'cause no one else

00:12:21.850 --> 00:12:23.460
is saying this shit."

00:12:23.460 --> 00:12:26.820
I just couldn't stop saying
it or like subconsciously.

00:12:26.820 --> 00:12:29.220
- I heard one thing that happens in Japan,

00:12:29.220 --> 00:12:30.290
now I don't expect this,

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:32.610
so I just noticed that it was different.

00:12:32.610 --> 00:12:33.900
When you get a taxI in Japan,

00:12:33.900 --> 00:12:35.290
the taxI driver will always try

00:12:35.290 --> 00:12:37.410
and help you with your bags,
even if you don't need it.

00:12:37.410 --> 00:12:38.662
- Right, yeah.

00:12:38.662 --> 00:12:39.495
- It could be the smallest bag on earth

00:12:39.495 --> 00:12:40.328
and he might try and come out

00:12:40.328 --> 00:12:41.990
and help you with it.

00:12:41.990 --> 00:12:45.170
And that never happened with
taxis in the UK I noticed.

00:12:45.170 --> 00:12:47.024
Every time I had a suitcase or anything,

00:12:47.024 --> 00:12:48.130
they would just sit in the car

00:12:48.130 --> 00:12:50.193
and then I'd have to open
the boot too as well,

00:12:50.193 --> 00:12:52.080
which is fine, totally fine with that.

00:12:52.080 --> 00:12:52.913
But I just felt, I was like,

00:12:52.913 --> 00:12:55.060
Oh yeah, this is the
thing that we don't do.

00:12:56.078 --> 00:12:56.911
And that was most of them.

00:12:56.911 --> 00:12:57.930
Yeah, that was the most it.

00:12:57.930 --> 00:12:59.090
- I'll tell you the biggest,

00:12:59.090 --> 00:13:03.610
the immediate thing that I noticed

00:13:03.610 --> 00:13:04.690
about going back to the UK,

00:13:04.690 --> 00:13:07.340
that I immediately missed about Japan.

00:13:07.340 --> 00:13:10.650
So, I think it was the second
day that we landed there

00:13:12.756 --> 00:13:13.820
and also my second day in the UK,

00:13:13.820 --> 00:13:17.770
and we went out to just
walk around Brighton again,

00:13:17.770 --> 00:13:20.500
walk around town, because
it's been quite a while

00:13:20.500 --> 00:13:21.610
since I've been in my hometown.

00:13:21.610 --> 00:13:25.150
So, me and Sydney just went
out, did a quick shopping trip

00:13:25.150 --> 00:13:27.690
and I got thirsty.

00:13:27.690 --> 00:13:30.410
My throat was a little bit parched, right?

00:13:30.410 --> 00:13:33.010
And so I started searching for a drink--

00:13:33.010 --> 00:13:34.090
- I can see where we are going with this.

00:13:34.090 --> 00:13:35.610
- In order to quench my thirst,

00:13:35.610 --> 00:13:37.523
and I look around and I'm just like,

00:13:38.707 --> 00:13:40.487
"Where are the vending machines?"

00:13:42.250 --> 00:13:45.670
How am I supposed to procure some liquids

00:13:45.670 --> 00:13:48.757
so that I may quench my thirst?"

00:13:48.757 --> 00:13:50.810
And I walk around in this shopping center

00:13:50.810 --> 00:13:52.100
and I'm like, wait.

00:13:52.100 --> 00:13:53.210
And then I soon realized,

00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:54.880
I have to go to a shop

00:13:54.880 --> 00:13:56.780
in order to purchase some liquids.

00:13:56.780 --> 00:13:58.540
- Not even a community
shop, like a supermarket.

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:00.867
- Yeah, like a corner shop.

00:14:00.867 --> 00:14:04.090
And I'm just like, "What
backward system is this?

00:14:04.090 --> 00:14:06.670
I need to go into a shop

00:14:06.670 --> 00:14:09.300
that sells other things other than drinks,

00:14:09.300 --> 00:14:12.260
and I need to do human interaction

00:14:12.260 --> 00:14:13.560
in order to purchase this?

00:14:14.493 --> 00:14:15.326
What is this?

00:14:15.326 --> 00:14:17.010
- We really do take for granted

00:14:17.010 --> 00:14:18.460
how many fucking vending machines

00:14:18.460 --> 00:14:19.993
are on this country.
- I didn't realize that,

00:14:19.993 --> 00:14:22.210
because sometimes, I'm
just walking around,

00:14:22.210 --> 00:14:23.300
I get a little thirsty,

00:14:23.300 --> 00:14:25.180
and I'm just like literally a minute away

00:14:25.180 --> 00:14:26.530
from the next vending machine.

00:14:26.530 --> 00:14:28.420
I didn't realize how convenient that was

00:14:28.420 --> 00:14:29.633
until I went to the UK.

00:14:31.007 --> 00:14:32.880
It's in a big shopping center,

00:14:32.880 --> 00:14:35.060
most of this is just like cloves,

00:14:35.060 --> 00:14:37.040
or different kinds of stores,

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:38.590
that are not like food drink items.

00:14:38.590 --> 00:14:39.910
And even if it is,

00:14:39.910 --> 00:14:42.380
most of them are something like super drug

00:14:42.380 --> 00:14:46.140
or some very big pharmacy store

00:14:46.140 --> 00:14:48.450
where they sell the drinks there,

00:14:48.450 --> 00:14:50.730
but it's way bigger than it needs to be.

00:14:50.730 --> 00:14:52.780
So I ended up like having to go in,

00:14:52.780 --> 00:14:54.080
go into this massive store,

00:14:54.080 --> 00:14:56.740
go to like the small segments,

00:14:56.740 --> 00:14:58.420
just for food and drink.

00:14:58.420 --> 00:14:59.792
- And then line up.
- And then line up.

00:14:59.792 --> 00:15:00.625
- Yeah.

00:15:00.625 --> 00:15:01.943
- And I'm just like--
- Disgusting.

00:15:01.943 --> 00:15:02.776
- Disgusting.

00:15:02.776 --> 00:15:04.730
- Imagine lining up for things.

00:15:04.730 --> 00:15:05.690
Not on my watch.

00:15:05.690 --> 00:15:07.773
- Imagine lining up for a bottle of water.

00:15:08.660 --> 00:15:09.824
Jesus!

00:15:09.824 --> 00:15:10.657
(all laughing)

00:15:10.657 --> 00:15:12.170
- But that's like the
first thing I noticed

00:15:12.170 --> 00:15:13.300
where it was just like,

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.340
I really missed the vending machines

00:15:15.340 --> 00:15:18.930
and just being able to
buy very conveniently,

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.850
buy like a snack or something .

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:23.400
- I felt that way as well,
whenever I got back to Australia,

00:15:23.400 --> 00:15:25.020
or like the U.S or anything as well.

00:15:25.020 --> 00:15:26.403
Because we have vending machines,

00:15:26.403 --> 00:15:28.067
like in Australia and the U.S

00:15:28.067 --> 00:15:30.580
but you don't have like a Suica.

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:31.413
- Yeah, like I said.

00:15:31.413 --> 00:15:33.770
- So you have to reach in,
grab your fucking change,

00:15:33.770 --> 00:15:35.750
jiggle that around and then--
- Yeah.

00:15:35.750 --> 00:15:36.583
- I don't know why

00:15:36.583 --> 00:15:38.800
they got you five fucking
vending machines in the West,

00:15:38.800 --> 00:15:42.297
because sometimes the drink
doesn't even fucking pull back.

00:15:42.297 --> 00:15:43.130
So I'm like, "Oh, good.

00:15:43.130 --> 00:15:44.950
I'm about to gamble $3

00:15:44.950 --> 00:15:46.346
to see if I can even get this.

00:15:46.346 --> 00:15:47.923
- Sometimes you gotta double-pull as well.

00:15:47.923 --> 00:15:49.710
That just happens sometimes.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:50.543
- You're really lucky for that.

00:15:50.543 --> 00:15:52.527
- Well, you buy a drink and you're like,

00:15:52.527 --> 00:15:53.900
"I'm gonna buy another drink immediately."

00:15:53.900 --> 00:15:55.420
But then, the machine is designed

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:56.980
in such a way that it just can't handle

00:15:56.980 --> 00:15:58.600
two drinks being dispensed at once.

00:15:58.600 --> 00:16:01.100
So you have to like fucking
scrunch your hand inside

00:16:01.100 --> 00:16:03.250
and pull one out, just so you can unblock

00:16:03.250 --> 00:16:04.640
this fucking thing.
- Yeah.

00:16:04.640 --> 00:16:07.250
- But, I noticed as well, obviously,

00:16:07.250 --> 00:16:08.847
I got at the airport and it's like,

00:16:08.847 --> 00:16:10.620
"No one was wearing masks."

00:16:10.620 --> 00:16:11.620
- Really?
- Yep, yep.

00:16:11.620 --> 00:16:13.253
- No one was wearing masks.

00:16:14.170 --> 00:16:15.510
I don't have fucking opinion on it.

00:16:15.510 --> 00:16:17.860
I do whatever I'm told to do, I'm a sheep.

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:19.160
All right, what can I say?

00:16:20.000 --> 00:16:20.833
- Wake up sheep!

00:16:20.833 --> 00:16:23.523
- That's taking a stance in the self club.

00:16:23.523 --> 00:16:24.370
- I really don't give
a shit, I'm just like,

00:16:24.370 --> 00:16:25.820
If everyone around me is wearing a mask,

00:16:25.820 --> 00:16:27.230
I'll wear a mask, you know what I mean?

00:16:27.230 --> 00:16:28.063
But it was one of those things

00:16:28.063 --> 00:16:28.896
where I was like,

00:16:28.896 --> 00:16:32.259
"Oh, whoa, this is fucking weird."

00:16:32.259 --> 00:16:35.780
'Cause in Japan, you'll be crucified

00:16:35.780 --> 00:16:36.762
if you don't wear a mask.

00:16:36.762 --> 00:16:37.740
