to what it normally would be.

00:36:48.490 --> 00:36:49.934
- Well, you also booked very late.

00:36:49.934 --> 00:36:50.767
- I did book very late.

00:36:50.767 --> 00:36:53.330
But even then, it was still
more expensive normally.

00:36:53.330 --> 00:36:54.400
And so, yeah.

00:36:54.400 --> 00:36:56.070
So I got this paper and I was like, great.

00:36:56.070 --> 00:36:59.400
I have this Japanese document
signed the way I think.

00:36:59.400 --> 00:37:01.960
I sent a message to Raina Sculley,

00:37:01.960 --> 00:37:04.190
who'd also done traveling abroad.

00:37:04.190 --> 00:37:06.310
And I was like, I sent her a
picture of the signed document.

00:37:06.310 --> 00:37:08.100
I'm like, "Does this look okay?"

00:37:08.100 --> 00:37:09.210
And she's like, "Yeah, it looks great."

00:37:09.210 --> 00:37:10.900
And I'm like, "Okay, good."

00:37:10.900 --> 00:37:12.357
But I had the next
appointment the next day

00:37:12.357 --> 00:37:13.267
for the people who were like,

00:37:13.267 --> 00:37:14.150
"Yeah, dude, it's all okay."

00:37:14.150 --> 00:37:16.720
And I'll get it signed there and all that.

00:37:16.720 --> 00:37:18.380
But to me it did make sense a little bit

00:37:18.380 --> 00:37:21.130
because I was like, "How are
you gonna sign all this stuff

00:37:21.130 --> 00:37:24.420
while I have to wait three
hours for the results?"

00:37:24.420 --> 00:37:26.540
So I turn up to this test the next day,

00:37:26.540 --> 00:37:27.373
'cause I'd already paid for it.

00:37:27.373 --> 00:37:28.640
So I was like, "Fuck it,
I'll just get another test,

00:37:28.640 --> 00:37:29.473
why not?"

00:37:30.920 --> 00:37:31.987
I go there, and I'm like,

00:37:31.987 --> 00:37:33.252
"Okay, so you need to fill out this form."

00:37:33.252 --> 00:37:34.477
They're like, "No."

00:37:34.477 --> 00:37:35.310
I'm like, "What do you mean?"

00:37:35.310 --> 00:37:36.740
They're like, "No, we
don't fill out that form."

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.377
I'm like, "I called you up

00:37:38.377 --> 00:37:39.670
and you said that you
could fill out this form."

00:37:39.670 --> 00:37:41.020
And they're like, "No, no,
no, no, no, we don't do that.

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:41.870
We will give you a phone."

00:37:41.870 --> 00:37:45.720
I'm like, "Yeah, but that's
not good enough for Japan.

00:37:45.720 --> 00:37:47.690
I told you this on the phone call

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:48.850
and you said it was no problem."

00:37:48.850 --> 00:37:50.123
And they were like, "No,
we're not doing that."

00:37:50.123 --> 00:37:52.082
- Did you not read the forum?

00:37:52.082 --> 00:37:54.012
(all laughing)

00:37:54.012 --> 00:37:55.107
- Did you not read the thread?

00:37:55.107 --> 00:37:57.310
And so then, a woman
came out of the office

00:37:57.310 --> 00:37:58.830
and she was like, "Oh
no, no, no, no, no, no.

00:37:58.830 --> 00:38:00.010
You're going to Japan?"

00:38:00.010 --> 00:38:01.020
She's like, "Oh yeah, come
with me, come with me.

00:38:01.020 --> 00:38:02.487
I got you, I got you."

00:38:02.487 --> 00:38:03.537
And so I go there and I'm like,

00:38:03.537 --> 00:38:04.671
"All right, so can you fill this out?"

00:38:04.671 --> 00:38:05.504
She's like, "No, no,
I'm not gonna fill out.'

00:38:05.504 --> 00:38:06.627
And I'm like,

00:38:06.627 --> 00:38:07.510
"Why aren't you gonna fill out?"

00:38:07.510 --> 00:38:08.343
She's like,

00:38:08.343 --> 00:38:09.176
"Oh, because the form I'm gonna give you

00:38:09.176 --> 00:38:10.220
is gonna be enough anyway.

00:38:10.220 --> 00:38:11.890
It's gonna have your
passport information on it."

00:38:11.890 --> 00:38:14.410
And I'm like, "All right."

00:38:14.410 --> 00:38:18.360
But I already knowing that
I had the signed document,

00:38:18.360 --> 00:38:19.193
put me at ease.

00:38:19.193 --> 00:38:21.100
I probably would have
been very uncomfortable,

00:38:21.100 --> 00:38:24.090
if I was in that situation
and didn't have a backup.

00:38:24.090 --> 00:38:26.960
And I'm glad I didn't,
because I got my test results

00:38:26.960 --> 00:38:29.882
after filling out this form and doing...

00:38:29.882 --> 00:38:32.140
They were like, "We
promise, it'll be fine.

00:38:32.140 --> 00:38:34.630
We promise this form will be enough."

00:38:34.630 --> 00:38:35.970
And so I'm like, "Okay, fine.

00:38:35.970 --> 00:38:36.810
All right, fine."

00:38:36.810 --> 00:38:38.670
So I get my results back later,

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:40.720
it's got even less information on it

00:38:40.720 --> 00:38:42.800
and no stamp, no doctor's signature.

00:38:42.800 --> 00:38:44.420
Then like the other test result I got

00:38:44.420 --> 00:38:46.750
to take to the doctor, which
wasn't even the real one.

00:38:46.750 --> 00:38:48.750
So they've given me
this piece of shit form,

00:38:48.750 --> 00:38:49.960
and then I went to the website,

00:38:49.960 --> 00:38:51.180
I should have done this before,

00:38:51.180 --> 00:38:54.100
and the reviews were
terrible for this clinic.

00:38:54.100 --> 00:38:56.650
And then I found out it was a clinic,

00:38:56.650 --> 00:39:01.513
but it was like a weird
modern medicine-esque clinic.

00:39:03.080 --> 00:39:05.410
But it was a legitimate COVID testing...

00:39:05.410 --> 00:39:07.427
They were allowed to do it.

00:39:07.427 --> 00:39:10.130
- But not for official
documents, I'm guessing.

00:39:10.130 --> 00:39:12.403
- Yeah, so then I was like, "All right,

00:39:14.007 --> 00:39:15.240
"I'm gonna see."

00:39:15.240 --> 00:39:17.330
'Cause now I have two forms.

00:39:17.330 --> 00:39:18.320
When I go to the airport,

00:39:18.320 --> 00:39:20.060
I'm like, "I'm gonna give
them the shit one first

00:39:20.060 --> 00:39:21.800
and see if they accept it."

00:39:21.800 --> 00:39:24.040
They probably thought I was
weird for doing this as well.

00:39:24.040 --> 00:39:25.580
So I give them the form,

00:39:25.580 --> 00:39:27.410
and then they looked really confused.

00:39:27.410 --> 00:39:28.540
They looked really uncomfortable

00:39:28.540 --> 00:39:29.950
and they brought like some staff over

00:39:29.950 --> 00:39:31.417
and I knew they were
about to tell me like,

00:39:31.417 --> 00:39:33.124
"You can't get on the flight with this."

00:39:33.124 --> 00:39:34.020
And I was like...

00:39:34.020 --> 00:39:35.040
And before they said anything to me,

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:37.320
I was like, 'By the way, I have
another form just in case."

00:39:37.320 --> 00:39:38.845
And they looked at me like,

00:39:38.845 --> 00:39:39.678
"What do you mean?"

00:39:39.678 --> 00:39:41.830
"Like I have like another test
with the form filled out."

00:39:41.830 --> 00:39:42.930
And they looked at me

00:39:42.930 --> 00:39:44.870
like I was doing something really bizarre.

00:39:44.870 --> 00:39:46.320
I imagined it would be.

00:39:46.320 --> 00:39:48.347
They were like, "Why
didn't you just give us?"

00:39:49.526 --> 00:39:51.520
Because I paid 150 bucks for this,

00:39:51.520 --> 00:39:53.670
I wanted to make sure
that it was bullshit.

00:39:55.190 --> 00:39:57.522
- This man limit-testing
the system right now.

00:39:57.522 --> 00:40:02.522
(group chattering)
(Garnt and Joey laughing)

00:40:05.014 --> 00:40:06.827
- I wanted to see if I'd been ripped off.

00:40:06.827 --> 00:40:09.560
And I realized now I played
paper please the other day,

00:40:09.560 --> 00:40:11.060
and I feel like, oh, this is
what I did in paper place.

00:40:11.060 --> 00:40:13.187
Some people just wouldn't give
that passport or something.

00:40:13.187 --> 00:40:14.020
But yeah.

00:40:16.124 --> 00:40:17.463
- Testing for bugs.

00:40:18.480 --> 00:40:20.130
- They didn't tell me they
weren't gonna accept it,

00:40:20.130 --> 00:40:23.060
but they were like bringing
managers over and stuff,

00:40:23.060 --> 00:40:24.330
after I handed them this form.

00:40:24.330 --> 00:40:25.163
And I was like,

00:40:26.417 --> 00:40:27.380
"Let's not worry.

00:40:27.380 --> 00:40:28.213
Let's not kick me out.

00:40:28.213 --> 00:40:29.046
Here's the real one.

00:40:29.046 --> 00:40:30.267
Here's the real one."

00:40:30.267 --> 00:40:32.070
- Yeah, because I talked
to like a few people

00:40:32.070 --> 00:40:34.610
who had traveled to their
home country and back to Japan

00:40:34.610 --> 00:40:35.840
before I even flew,

00:40:35.840 --> 00:40:39.120
and that's how I knew
about this quarantine hotel

00:40:39.120 --> 00:40:40.377
that we were going to have to stay in.

00:40:40.377 --> 00:40:44.167
And we'll get to that, but
every one of them told me,

00:40:44.167 --> 00:40:47.010
"You need to get this form,

00:40:47.010 --> 00:40:49.940
this form that the Japanese
government gIve you, signed.

00:40:49.940 --> 00:40:51.570
Doesn't matter what any clinic says,

00:40:51.570 --> 00:40:53.710
doesn't matter what they promise,

00:40:53.710 --> 00:40:55.270
if you don't have this form,

00:40:55.270 --> 00:40:58.710
you're not gonna guaranteed
to get back here."

00:40:58.710 --> 00:41:01.530
And so you have to look
for a specific clinic

00:41:01.530 --> 00:41:04.450
that can fill out this
exact form with a doctor,

00:41:04.450 --> 00:41:05.820
which is a lot harder to find

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:08.760
than you originally think it is.

00:41:08.760 --> 00:41:10.460
There's a big difference

00:41:10.460 --> 00:41:12.710
between getting a positive COVID test,

00:41:12.710 --> 00:41:14.810
which is all you need to get out of Japan,

00:41:14.810 --> 00:41:15.850
but getting back into Japan,

00:41:15.850 --> 00:41:17.870
you need to have this exact form

00:41:17.870 --> 00:41:19.623
in order to get back in Japan.

00:41:20.510 --> 00:41:23.083
And luckily, Connor went
a few days before me,

00:41:24.109 --> 00:41:26.240
so I had like my own clinic booked.

00:41:26.240 --> 00:41:28.050
And hearing about his second experience,

00:41:28.050 --> 00:41:31.800
I'm just like, "Yeah, I'm just
gonna go for the proven way.

00:41:31.800 --> 00:41:34.457
Yeah, let Connor be my beta tester."

00:41:35.350 --> 00:41:37.090
- It was just like, it was so stressful.

00:41:37.090 --> 00:41:41.270
I was just trying to get a
goddamn piece of paper signed

00:41:41.270 --> 00:41:42.620
and it was like impossible,

00:41:42.620 --> 00:41:45.190
but luckily, I managed to do it.

00:41:45.190 --> 00:41:48.010
I don't know how it would be in the US,

00:41:48.010 --> 00:41:50.140
I just know that getting
doctor appointments in the UK

00:41:50.140 --> 00:41:51.760
is quite difficult unless
you're spending money,

00:41:51.760 --> 00:41:53.330
like a lot of money.

00:41:53.330 --> 00:41:56.160
So, and again, it's kind of
come under a lot of criticism,

00:41:56.160 --> 00:41:57.690
rightfully so, 'cause traveling,

00:41:57.690 --> 00:41:59.520
even though it's a luxury in general,

00:41:59.520 --> 00:42:02.343
but they've made it ridiculously
expensive to travel.

00:42:03.900 --> 00:42:07.460
I probably spent like 600, 500
bucks on COVID tests alone,

00:42:07.460 --> 00:42:10.300
which I'm lucky enough to
be able to afford that,

00:42:10.300 --> 00:42:12.383
but it's pretty fucked
that it's that much.

00:42:12.383 --> 00:42:14.030
You know what I mean?

00:42:14.030 --> 00:42:15.230
It's weird.

00:42:15.230 --> 00:42:16.180
It's annoying.

00:42:16.180 --> 00:42:17.220
So then yeah, got on the plane

00:42:17.220 --> 00:42:19.230
and then it almost felt too easy,

00:42:19.230 --> 00:42:21.810
'cause I got through check-in
and it was super easy.

00:42:21.810 --> 00:42:23.050
Was it easy for you as well?

00:42:23.050 --> 00:42:23.883
- Yeah.

00:42:23.883 --> 00:42:25.240
It was very easy-
- Show them the paper,

00:42:25.240 --> 00:42:27.982
they were like, "Yeah, have fun."

00:42:27.982 --> 00:42:31.230
- Once you show the paper,
which was the same for me,

00:42:31.230 --> 00:42:33.420
like going out of the country,
once you show the paper

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:34.860
and everything's accepted,

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:38.250
then it's the most stress-less experience

00:42:38.250 --> 00:42:40.760
I've had in an airport,
because it's fucking empty,

00:42:40.760 --> 00:42:42.250
'cause no one's traveling.

00:42:42.250 --> 00:42:45.980
So you're like, no queue
for security or anything,

00:42:45.980 --> 00:42:47.423
you just walked straight in,

00:42:48.357 --> 00:42:49.993
and it's just a breeze.

00:42:50.853 --> 00:42:52.640
Like after everything got accepted,

00:42:52.640 --> 00:42:55.900
I've never had a more pleasant
experience in an airport.

00:42:55.900 --> 00:42:57.930
And I never thought we'd
get a good experience

00:42:57.930 --> 00:43:01.230
in an airport, until now.

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:02.063
- It was really chill.

00:43:02.063 --> 00:43:03.450
I had a great time at the airport.

00:43:03.450 --> 00:43:05.433
I was charging my devices.

00:43:06.580 --> 00:43:09.720
In my terminal, I think I
counted, there was like 20 people

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:11.520
in the whole waiting area.

00:43:11.520 --> 00:43:12.353
- Wow!

00:43:12.353 --> 00:43:13.540
- For like the whole thing.

00:43:13.540 --> 00:43:17.113
So you had your whole section to yourself.

00:43:18.380 --> 00:43:21.580
- Yeah, but then it came to
coming back into the Japan,

00:43:21.580 --> 00:43:24.130
and we always obviously
talked about the documents.

00:43:26.330 --> 00:43:28.553
That was just the start of the process.

00:43:28.553 --> 00:43:29.880
Because you get off the plane,

00:43:29.880 --> 00:43:31.760
and obviously you have to
present the same documents

00:43:31.760 --> 00:43:33.721
and everything like that.

00:43:33.721 --> 00:43:38.721
And they put you in this
queue of just checkpoints.

00:43:39.737 --> 00:43:41.450
- [Connor] It was like eight checkpoints,

00:43:41.450 --> 00:43:42.853
that's before immigration.

00:43:43.932 --> 00:43:46.240
So you do eight checkpoints
before you can even

00:43:46.240 --> 00:43:48.140
get to what is normally the annoying part.

00:43:48.140 --> 00:43:49.160
- Yeah, yeah.

00:43:49.160 --> 00:43:52.790
- And you kind of like, you go in

00:43:52.790 --> 00:43:55.407
and they present you these documents,

00:43:55.407 --> 00:43:57.130
and then they give you a tag,

00:43:57.130 --> 00:44:01.653
that you have to put over
your hand to identify you.

00:44:02.917 --> 00:44:04.562
- It's like a fucking slaughterhouse.

00:44:04.562 --> 00:44:05.780
- That is exactly it.

00:44:05.780 --> 00:44:09.100
So you're literally like being
cattle, being herded through.

00:44:09.100 --> 00:44:11.840
- It's how they know you're
going to the quarantine hotel.

00:44:11.840 --> 00:44:16.286
They put this large thing
on your hand like this.

00:44:16.286 --> 00:44:18.193
- [Garnt] They literally tag you.

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:21.747
- I took it off for a second
and someone shouted at me,

00:44:21.747 --> 00:44:24.297
'cause I wanted to get my
coat off and I took it off

00:44:24.297 --> 00:44:26.760
and they were like, two men came over,

00:44:26.760 --> 00:44:28.320
shouting and panicking at me,

00:44:28.320 --> 00:44:29.808
pointing at it to put it back on.

00:44:29.808 --> 00:44:31.630
And I was like, "What the fuck man?

00:44:31.630 --> 00:44:33.740
Give me a fucking second.

00:44:33.740 --> 00:44:37.360
- So, they give you about
17 million documents,

00:44:37.360 --> 00:44:41.040
that they will check
across like this journey

00:44:41.040 --> 00:44:42.240
that you take.

00:44:42.240 --> 00:44:44.560
And the thing is like,
you present this document,

00:44:44.560 --> 00:44:46.343
they check it, great, go
to the next checkpoint.

00:44:46.343 --> 00:44:49.630
Then also the same
document, and they check it,

00:44:49.630 --> 00:44:50.463
you go to the next point.

00:44:50.463 --> 00:44:51.765
And for some reason,

00:44:51.765 --> 00:44:54.250
these few documents that you have,

00:44:54.250 --> 00:44:57.303
get checked a total about like five times,

00:44:58.270 --> 00:45:00.490
by different checkpoints,
even though nothing changed

00:45:00.490 --> 00:45:02.750
between you walking
from point A to point B.

00:45:02.750 --> 00:45:03.910
- There's like eight stops.

00:45:03.910 --> 00:45:05.810
So the first stop is they...

00:45:05.810 --> 00:45:07.060
So this is also a thing, right?

00:45:07.060 --> 00:45:07.950
No one told me this.

00:45:07.950 --> 00:45:10.480
You have to keep your boarding card.

00:45:10.480 --> 00:45:11.410
Normally on airplanes,

00:45:11.410 --> 00:45:12.243
I just like...

00:45:12.243 --> 00:45:13.500
Fuck it, the moment I'm on the plane,

00:45:13.500 --> 00:45:15.260
I just put the boarding card in my pocket,

00:45:15.260 --> 00:45:17.200
scrupled up or something.

00:45:17.200 --> 00:45:18.133
- Really?

00:45:18.133 --> 00:45:19.930
- Because you don't it need
any more, you're on the plane.

00:45:19.930 --> 00:45:21.980
- Yeah you do, the luggage tags.

00:45:21.980 --> 00:45:23.000
What do you do with the luggage tags?

00:45:23.000 --> 00:45:24.140
'cause that's normally on your card.

00:45:24.140 --> 00:45:24.973
- Oh no.

00:45:24.973 --> 00:45:25.970
They normally put that on
your passport or something.

00:45:25.970 --> 00:45:27.307
- Oh really?
- Yeah.

00:45:27.307 --> 00:45:28.470
But the luggage tags...

00:45:28.470 --> 00:45:29.900
I know my bag, I've never had to use it.

00:45:29.900 --> 00:45:32.040
- I always hold onto them
until I'm out at the airport.

00:45:32.040 --> 00:45:33.160
- Oh, the luggage tags, I keep.

00:45:33.160 --> 00:45:34.150
But normally the boarding card,

00:45:34.150 --> 00:45:36.197
I would just scruple it
in my pocket or something.

00:45:36.197 --> 00:45:37.030
- Oh really?

00:45:37.030 --> 00:45:37.863
- No one told me you needed it,

00:45:37.863 --> 00:45:40.680
because you never need it at an airport.

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:41.950
- I just hold onto it just in case.

00:45:41.950 --> 00:45:42.783
- Just in case.

00:45:42.783 --> 00:45:43.730
- I don't throw it away.

00:45:43.730 --> 00:45:45.470
I'm not like on the plane,
fucking tearing it up,

00:45:45.470 --> 00:45:47.780
but I don't think about it much.

00:45:47.780 --> 00:45:50.030
I'm kind of just like whatever,
but yeah, you needed it.

00:45:50.030 --> 00:45:50.900
So when you get off the plane,

00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:52.440
they ask for like your passport,

00:45:52.440 --> 00:45:54.250
your boarding pass and the documents.

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:56.140
Now, I printed off all the documents

00:45:56.140 --> 00:45:58.240
that the Japanese
government told me to have.

00:45:58.240 --> 00:45:59.220
Turns out that some of them,

00:45:59.220 --> 00:46:00.600
they just give you on the plane anyway.

00:46:00.600 --> 00:46:01.433
- Yeah.

00:46:01.433 --> 00:46:02.280
- So I didn't need to print them out.

00:46:02.280 --> 00:46:04.430
So I didn't even end up using my version.

00:46:04.430 --> 00:46:06.990
I used the ones that
they printed out for me,

00:46:06.990 --> 00:46:08.090
'cause it was like smaller,

00:46:08.090 --> 00:46:10.849
and the one I printed out
was A4 and it was like a...

00:46:10.849 --> 00:46:12.160
I don't really understand.

00:46:12.160 --> 00:46:13.380
So, you have these two documents.

00:46:13.380 --> 00:46:16.520
One of them is a declaration
saying that you're a good boy

00:46:16.520 --> 00:46:17.530
and you won't leave your house

00:46:17.530 --> 00:46:18.607
and you won't break quarantine rules.

00:46:18.607 --> 00:46:20.307
And if you do, they'll deport you.

00:46:22.180 --> 00:46:23.325
- Not ominous at all?

00:46:23.325 --> 00:46:24.220
- And if you're a Japanese national,

00:46:24.220 --> 00:46:26.370
I think they will do criminal
